  "titleEnglish": "Introductions",
  "language": "French",
  "description": "A short introduction dialogue for names, origins, nationalities, and the main present-tense forms of être.",
  "notes": {
    "1": "<strong>appeler</strong> means <strong>to call</strong>. <strong>Je m'appelle</strong> literally means <strong>I call myself</strong>, but is equivalent to <strong>My name is...</strong>",
    "2": "A formal but simple way to form a question with <strong>être</strong> is to switch the verb and pronoun: <strong>Vous êtes Breton ?</strong> becomes <strong>Êtes-vous Breton ?</strong>",
    "3": "Some nouns and adjectives describing men and women have two forms. <strong>bretonne</strong> is the feminine form of <strong>breton</strong>.",
    "4": "The first-person pronoun <strong>je</strong>, <strong>I</strong>, does not take an initial capital unless it is the first word of the sentence.",
    "5": "Now you know the main forms of <strong>être</strong>: <strong>je suis</strong>, <strong>il est</strong>, <strong>vous êtes</strong>, <strong>nous sommes</strong>: <strong>I am</strong>, <strong>he is</strong>, <strong>you are</strong>, <strong>we are</strong>."
  },
